      "module_id": "39646212-c555-43d5-a403-a79e237803e4",
      "title": "Module 1 Introduction — Discover & Define",
      "slug": "module-one-introduction",
      "instructions": "# Welcome to Module 1: Discover & Define\n\nYou may have several interests and questions worth exploring. This module will help you narrow those possibilities without losing the reasons they matter to you. You will learn three lenses—relevance, feasibility and scope—then observe an example, try guided decisions, and compare two possibilities for your own project. After feedback, you'll submit a Project Direction Brief. The goal is a reasoned choice, not a perfect answer.\n\n## Assigned reading — Three Lenses, One Decision\n\nAn interest is a starting point, not yet a project plan. “I like art,” for example, can lead to many directions: learning a drawing technique, making illustrations to explain a topic, or designing a small display for a school club. Your task is not to prove that one of these is objectively the best idea. It is to make a choice you can explain.\n\n**Relevance:** Why does this direction matter to you or to a community you care about? Personal relevance can come from curiosity, usefulness, creativity, or a question you'd like to answer. A meaningful reason doesn't need to be dramatic.\n\n**Feasibility:** What would it take to begin? Consider time, supplies, permissions, information and support. A project can matter a great deal and still be difficult to start right now. That doesn't make the idea bad; it may suggest a smaller first step.\n\n**Scope:** How big is the idea? “Improve school lunches” is broad. “Ask five willing students which lunch menu information is hardest to find, without collecting sensitive personal details” is narrower and creates a specific question. Focus lets you gather evidence without claiming to solve the whole problem at once.\n\nA **tradeoff** is something you accept when choosing one option over another. Maybe an idea is personally exciting but requires materials you don't have. Another idea may be less ambitious but easier to test. A strong decision recognizes both strengths and limitations.\n\n**Try this:** Write two possible directions. For each, name one reason it matters, one constraint, and one way to narrow it. Which direction would you investigate first, and what could make you reconsider?\n\nUse this reading to revisit the three decision lenses. The interactive lesson and Gauntlet will give you practice, and the Project Direction Brief will show how you use the lenses for your own idea.\n\nContinue to Your Starting Point. Return to this reading whenever you need support.\n\n### By the end of this module\n\n- Identify at least two interests or questions that could become a project.\n- Apply relevance, feasibility, and scope to compare two possible directions.\n- Justify a focused direction and explain a tradeoff in your Project Direction Brief.\n\nAfter the worked lesson, try the Gauntlet or use the included text case in Compare Two Directions. Carry a specific question from your Direction Brief into Module 2.\n\n### Plan your week\n\nDay 1 is when your facilitator opens this module. Share Compare Two Directions for formative feedback by Day 3. Submit the Project Direction Brief by Day 7 after considering any feedback. Check Course questions in Discussions for help with timing.",
