      "module_id": "9f62f101-6940-4084-8b32-b4e973cfd4f4",
      "title": "Module 2 Introduction — Plan & Launch",
      "slug": "module-two-introduction",
      "instructions": "# Welcome to Module 2: Plan & Launch\n\nYou chose a direction. Now you'll turn it into a manageable first move. You'll learn planning concepts, study Maya's school-garden example, practice recognizing safe actions and useful evidence, and enter First Move Studio to apply the same ideas to a new situation. You'll create a First Move Card, receive feedback, and decide whether to revise or retain your plan. The Launch Portfolio will show what you tried—or the honest barrier you encountered—and the next decision you can justify.\n\n## Assigned reading — An Informative First Move\n\nA first action does not need to complete a project. Its job is to help you learn something useful while staying safe and realistic.\n\nBegin with your **purpose**: what are you trying to improve, create or understand? Next, identify **milestones**—at least three ordered stages that take you from preparation toward a possible outcome. A milestone tells you what progress could look like, not what must happen no matter what.\n\nThen list **resources** and **constraints**. Resources might include time, information, a workspace or permission from a responsible adult. Constraints are things that may limit your action: a busy schedule, unavailable materials, privacy concerns or school rules. A thoughtful plan includes what you would do if a constraint matters.\n\nChoose one **bounded action**. It should be small enough to do safely and informative enough to help you decide what comes next. For a school garden, the bounded action may be to ask a staff sponsor what permission is needed and sketch a possible single bed, not to start digging without permission.\n\nFinally, distinguish **prediction** from **evidence**. “I think students will like my idea” is a prediction. “Three people I asked could explain the draft clearly” is an observation, though the observation still cannot prove that everyone will like it. Be specific about what you actually learned and what remains uncertain.\n\nWhen feedback arrives, you do not have to agree automatically. Consider the comment, ask whether evidence supports it, and either revise your decision or explain why you are keeping it.\n\n**Try this:** For a project you care about, write one safe first action, one thing that could stop it, one possible observation and one thing that observation would **not** prove.\n\nRead this before Maya's worked example and the guided checks. Your actions must be safe, bounded and honest.\n\n### By the end of this module\n\n- Analyze how a purpose, ordered milestones, and constraints shape a plan.\n- Develop and revise a safe first move using facilitator feedback and actual evidence or an honest account of a barrier.\n- Explain what you learned, what remains uncertain, and one justified next move in your Launch Portfolio.\n\nFollow the module in order. Share the First Move Card with your facilitator. Return for the feedback before completing Respond to Feedback and acting on your plan.\n\n### Plan your week\n\nDay 1 is when your facilitator opens this module. Share your First Move Card by Day 3. Review the facilitator’s feedback and complete your revision or retention note by Day 5. Submit your Launch Portfolio by Day 7 after documenting a safe attempt or a genuine barrier. If feedback is late, save your draft and ask the facilitator how to proceed.",
